H.265 on NVENC 5.0 (T210 nvenc.bin, app id 3): what the firmware reads, and the values NVIDIA's T210 driver gives it.
Used by TegraNVENCCheckH265.h and SwitchVideoEncoders/AVE/HEVCNVENC.c; test/hevcnvenc_host.c checks the IDR and P
setups byte for byte against the driver's at three sizes.

Firmware (TegraNVENC/firmware/nvidia/tegra210/nvenc.bin, read with tools/falcon; addresses are image offsets = falcon
code addresses). The OS (0x0-0x500, overlay 0x500-0xc00) picks app 3 from SET_APPLICATION_ID at 0x619: code
0x1f00-0x2e00 loaded and entered at 0x1f00, data image 0x1a600 (0x1400 bytes) to DMEM 0x200. The app copies the
method values to DMEM 0x3b8 (words 0 and 1 at 0x3b8 and 0x3bc, word k = method 0x700 + 4k at 0x3b4 + 4k from word 3,
0xf41a-0xf4bb; SET_IN_DRV_PIC_SETUP at 0x3c4).
  - Setup: 4 x 256 bytes from SET_IN_DRV_PIC_SETUP to DMEM 0xa00-0xe00 (0x1f52-0x1f79, again 0x13b29-0x13b50): the
    1024-byte nvenc_h265_drv_pic_setup_s. Magic check 0x14a84 (0xd0b70006, NV_NVENC_5_0; else error 0xe2640020).
  - Controls: ME 3 x 64 bytes from me_control_offset (setup+0x190) to DMEM 0x700 (0x13b6d-0x13b8d; per slice 0xf9fd by
    me_control_idx), MD 128 bytes from md_control_offset (+0x194) to 0x800 (0x13b9e; per slice 0xf9d2), the slice
    control 64 bytes a slice to 0x7c0 (0xf9af). q_control_offset (+0x198) is copied to DMEM 0x63c (0x1474b) and never
    read: the quant block is not fetched. A transfer needs its offset aligned to its size (falcon xfer rules).
  - Every setup field the app reads, with its code addresses:
      bazel run //tools/falcon -- accesses $PWD/drivers/TegraNVENC/firmware/nvidia/tegra210/nvenc.bin \
          --entry 0x1f00 --region 0xa00:0x400
    (0x700:0xc0, 0x7c0:0x40 and 0x800:0x80 for the controls). The reads land on nvenc_drv.h's H.265 field boundaries
    throughout (through act_stat_offset at +0x2e4): the C9B7 layout is NVENC 5.0's. gpTimer_timeout_val (+0x30c) and
    wp_control_offset (+0x2b0) are never read.
  - CONTROL_PARAMS: the codec type is unread. GPTIMER_ON (bit 12, copied to DMEM 0x378 at 0xf4ef) arms the GP timer
    with width x height x 24 cycles, at most 0x1600000 (0x1f2a-0x1fb6); its interrupt stores error 0xe2640010 (ISR 0x26f5). RCSTAT_READ/WRITE (bits
    20/19) gate the RC data transfers (0x11b5c, 0x108dc); with both off SET_IN_RCDATA is never used.
  - End of picture (fn 0xfcf5): a wait that ends on an error or engine idle, then an unbounded spin at 0xfd35 until
    the CTBs the ISR counts (DMEM 0x360 += I[0x1848], 0x2776) reach the picture's (DMEM 0x38c, fn 0xfe8b), then
    unbounded polls of I[0x1964], I[0x4c] and I[0x410]. A stalled engine hangs the app there whatever the GP timer
    does. The H.264 app has the same spin (fn 0x48b9, 0x4916), its target the slice control's num_mb (0x38df) clamped
    to the picture (0x38f7-0x390e; SOURCE).
  - Reference lists: l0[0..3] go to I/O 0x1884 as entry bit 0 and entry bits 1-4 per byte (0x143dd-0x1442e): an entry
    is DPB slot << 1, the driver's -2 (0xfe) unused. pic_type: (pic_type + 2) & 3 > 1 is inter (0x14208).
  - Surfaces: the app copies method words 0-0x12 only (0xf41a-0xf4bb, DMEM 0x3b8 + 4 x (word - 1) from word 3; 0x400
    cleared). The input is IN_CUR_PIC and CHROMA_U plus input_cfg's offsets (0x141a2-0x141cd); the reconstruction is
    OUT_REF_PIC_LUMA plus outputpic_cfg's luma and chroma offsets (I[0x1878], I[0x1880], 0x142da-0x142fe); references
    are the 16 IN_REF_PIC<n>_LUMA words (0x13-0x22, all to I[0x1040-0x107c] at 0x13c5f-0x13c74) plus refpic_cfg's
    offsets (0x14267-0x142a4). OUT_REF_PIC_CHROMA and IN_REF_PIC<n>_CHROMA are never read, so a reconstruction's chroma
    is always at its config offset.

Driver: L4T 32.7.6 nvidia-l4t-multimedia for t210 (repo.download.nvidia.com/jetson/t210,
nvidia-l4t-multimedia_32.7.6-20241104234540_arm64.deb, sha256
e85b22ac1988e7e8926258efd7a54b16fa4fcdbb15810b73dc817570524f0d7b), its libnvtvmr.so (sha256
db8f49f52d74032207cc3c9f456da88408def79de8631d29ca93cdb18b339980), read with llvm-objdump -d; none of it was run.
The NVENC magic is built three times (movk #0xd0b7): 0x31128 (TVMR codec 0, H.264), 0x3a7e0 (codec 9, H.265),
0x47808 (codec 8); each stores it only for chip version 6 (T210; the global at .bss 0x90f0c).
  - H.265 create 0x39f38: per setup buffer a zeroed 0x1100-byte allocation (0x3a74c-0x3a7ac) laid out setup 0x000,
    slice controls 0x400, MD 0xc00, quant 0xd00, ME 0xe00, WP 0xf00 (0x3a784-0x3a7a8; offsets stored 0x3a9c4-0x3a9c8),
    defaults 0x3a7e8-0x3aa74 (T210 surfaces 0x3b420). Frame 0x3d378 (feed 0x40ca8): sizes, picture type and slice
    controls (0x3e4f8-0x3f530); quality presets 0x3ca20 (T210 0x3cc54, quality level 0 at 0x3ce5c); level 0x37b98;
    CONTROL_PARAMS 0x3f12c-0x3f1a4 (codec type 3 | FORCE_OUTPIC | SLICE_STAT_ON | rc mode << 24); lists
    0x3e504-0x3e520 (IDR) and 0x3fb1c-0x3fb28 (slot << 1, temporal distance = POC difference).
  - Methods per picture (0x3f12c-0x3f8b0): CONTROL_PARAMS, PICTURE_INDEX, APPLICATION_ID 3, DRV_PIC_SETUP,
    ENC_STATUS, BITSTREAM, IOHISTORY, IO_RC_PROCESS (a 256-byte buffer, 0x3a694), IN_CUR_PIC, CHROMA_U, CHROMA_V, then
    for P and B only (NvMedia type <= 2, 0x3f370) the ME predictor pair and the references, then OUT_REF_PIC_LUMA and
    EXECUTE. An IDR has no ME predictors; app 3 turns predictor input and output off for a 0 address (I[0x910],
    I[0x984] at 0x114e9-0x11521), which is what TegraNVENC writes when a submit names none.
  - The values: those functions followed with a zeroed configuration (no feature flags, quality level 0, rate control
    mode 0, which a zero bitrate forces to constant QP, NvMedia picture type 0 = automatic, chip version 6) for the
    first (IDR) picture at 1280x720, 1920x1080 and 640x360, 30 fps, by static symbolic evaluation of the disassembly
    with branches on unknown configuration decided by hand. Only the coded size, the CTB count (max_slice_size,
    num_ctus, ceil_log2_pic_size_in_ctbs), hist_buf_size (width/16 x (height/16 + 1) x 192) and bitstream_buf_size
    (width/16 x height/16 x 768, at least 0x8000) change with size, all for 32-aligned coded sizes; the ME, MD and quant
    controls are the same at every size.
  - Check of the reading: the same evaluation of the H.264 path (create 0x30950, frame 0x2ce58) gives the quant control
    NVIDIA's HOS driver sent on a Switch (SOURCE, switch-frame-tap) byte for byte, and its ME and MD controls in every
    byte NVENC 5.0's H.264 app reads except fields the configuration sets (8x8 transform and profile, MV limits from
    the level, sub-8x8 partitions).

P pictures (the driver's DPB, fn 0x45490, an HM-style reference picture manager, evaluated with memset and memcpy
modelled, for the IDR then two P pictures at the three sizes; the two branches in its RPS builder decided by hand,
0x445b0 and 0x44c2c, are stack-protector checks):
  - DPB: two slots at the default of one reference (ctx+0x2ec + 1, 0x46160); picture POC n is reconstructed in slot
    n & 1 (OUT_REF_PIC_LUMA, 0x4066c) and a P picture's list is its predecessor's slot (ctx+0x121a8, 0x45cd0-0x45d0c).
  - Setup (only these bytes differ from the IDR's, at every size; the ME, MD and quant controls do not): l0[0] =
    slot << 1, temp_dist_l0[0] = POC difference 1 (0x3fa9c-0x3fb34); dist_scale_factor 256 in every entry (0x402b8:
    (16384 + |td| / 2) / td x td); POC LSB; pic_type 0 with ref_pic_flag (0x3e638-0x3e658); NAL type 1, TRAIL_R
    (ctx+0x10d54, 0x455d8-0x455e8); short_term_ref_pic_set_sps_flag 1, index 0 (0x3e6e0-0x3e6ec), the slice's own RPS
    left 0 (0x3e780); NumPocTotalCurr 1 (0x3e950-0x3e984); the slice control's QP the P QP (I + 2, 0x3f510),
    num_ref_idx_active_override_flag 0 (the PPS default's count, 0x40524) and slice_type 0.
  - slice_type: the driver gives 0 for P and 1 for B (0x3eb9c-0x3ebd4, 0x3f510), its pic_type numbering, which its DPB
    code uses throughout (ctx+0x10d70: 0 P, 1 B, 2 I, 3 IDR; list 1 built for 1 only, 0x45d10). nvenc_drv.h numbers
    the field as H.265 does (0 B, 1 P). App 3 passes it to I[0xa28] bits 6-7 unread (0x10bc4, 0x1041b). Which slice
    type NVENC writes into the stream is not established; with this SPS and PPS either decodes (a B slice's list 1 is
    the same one-picture RPS, num_ref_idx_l1_default 1).
  - Methods: per list-0 entry IN_REF_PIC<slot>_LUMA only on chip 6 (0x3fd28, 0x3fedc, 0x3fe0c); the ME predictor pair
    in k, out k ^ 1, k flipped each P picture (0x403d4-0x40410, 0x403c4); PICTURE_INDEX pictures since the session began
    (ctx+0x10a1c, incremented after the submit, 0x3f978). The driver's predictor buffers: (width/16 + 3 & ~3) x
    (height/16 + 1) x 4 bytes, 256-aligned (0x3a558-0x3a5e0); the RC process buffer 256 bytes (0x3a694).
  - NVENC writes the slice segment header itself: I[0x1800] picture type, IDR bit, NAL type and temporal id
    (0x13c7d-0x13cc8), I[0x1810] POC LSB and picture index (0x13e36-0x13e4d), I[0x180c] POC LSB length (0x146ee),
    I[0x181c] num_ref_idx override and counts (0x10b9f-0x10bbd), I[0x1978] RPS flag, index and NumPocTotalCurr
    (0x14540-0x145a6), I[0x1ae4] and I[0x1b28-0x1b3c] the slice's RPS, long-term and list-modification tables
    (0x145ad-0x146e7). AVEEncoder.c sends NVENC's bitstream as it is; HEVCEncSyntax.c's slice header is its model,
    checked against the setup in test/hevcnvenc_host.c, not written to the stream.

HEVCNVENC.c differs from the driver where it says: its own control offsets and buffers, the GP timer on,
wp_control_offset 0 and gpTimer_timeout_val set (both unread), the rate control QPs from the picture's (the driver's
I, I + 2, I + 5; app 3 reads the block only under rate control, 0x1493d), the DPB slot and ME predictor buffer taken
from the POC (the driver's predictor index runs on through IDRs), one reference always.

github.com/mickys/nvenc (MIT) src/hevcenctmpl.h, NVIDIA's desktop driver's HEVC blocks for NVENC 7 and later, agrees
with this driver's chip > 7 branch (0x3cabc: shape 0 0xff0c0c00/0x0c0c) and has T210's quant bytes (6b 05 ff aa aa ff
0f 00 0f 0f 0f); its ME and MD presets are not T210's.
